string getEmployeeReport(string dateTxt)
{
	// ONE DAY OF THE FLOOR, one row per person.
	// POST: dateTxt - the day, as "dd-MMM-yyyy". Empty means today.
	//
	// Replaces getOperatorPerformance, getDailyProductionTrend,
	// getRecentActivities and getOrderPipelineCounts with a single call. Those
	// four were re-run together every ten seconds; this is one call, on demand.
	//
	// Dates arrive pre-formatted because Deluge parses "dd-MMM-yyyy" the same way
	// whatever the org's locale is, while "08-11-2026" is a different day in the
	// US than it is here.
	//
	//-------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// WHAT "EFFICIENCY" WAS, AND WHY THIS FUNCTION WILL NOT SEND ONE.
	//
	// getOperatorPerformance summed Qty_In into a field called "Target" and
	// divided Qty_Out by it. There is no target anywhere in this app - that was
	// the pieces the operator was HANDED. So the figure was yield, not
	// productivity, and saveProductionPhase caps Qty_Out at Qty_In ("you cannot
	// finish more than you received"), which bounds it at 100 by construction.
	//
	// The effect was that a man who cut 500 pieces cleanly and a man who cut 5
	// cleanly both read 100%, and both counted as "target met". Volume - the
	// thing actually being asked about - had no card at all.
	//
	// So pieces in, pieces out and the loss between them are sent as the plain
	// counts they are. The widget shows yield as its own column, named yield,
	// next to the volume that gives it meaning. Nothing here is called
	// efficiency, because nothing here measures it.
	//
	// "stagesDone" and "stagesOpen" count a person's SHARES of stages, not
	// stages. Three men on one cutting stage is three rows here and one stage on
	// the floor - the report is about people, so the row each person can see
	// themselves in is the unit. Only a stage nobody was assigned to is counted
	// as itself.
	//
	// Status is sent from Employee.Status and nowhere else. The old function
	// wrote the real value into the map and then overwrote it with a badge
	// derived from that same ratio - so "On Break" meant "lost some pieces" and
	// "Offline" meant "no finished stage today", and the real Active/Inactive
	// flag was computed and thrown away.
	//-------------------------------------------------------------------------
	//
	// EVERY LOOKUP IS CACHED, and the employee directory is fetched ONCE rather
	// than queried per person. The old function ran a Stage_Log query per
	// employee - cost growing with headcount instead of with work done - which is
	// the shape that trips the statement-execution limit. That limit is NOT
	// catchable: it kills the script, so the try/catch never runs and the widget
	// gets a bare 500 with no error card to explain it.

	workedJson = "";
	idleJson = "";
	errJson = "";
	dayTxt = "";
	totPeople = 0;
	totStagesDone = 0;
	totStagesOpen = 0;
	totIn = 0;
	totOpen = 0;
	totOut = 0;
	pendingCount = 0;
	inProgressCount = 0;
	completedCount = 0;
	qcCount = 0;
	packedCount = 0;
	dispatchedCount = 0;

	// This summary must not depend on the selected day's Stage_Log report.
	// If an employee/report query below has a problem, the dashboard can still
	// show the current, live Sales_Order status totals.
	try
	{
		pendingCount = Sales_Order[Order_Status == "Pending"].count();
		inProgressCount = Sales_Order[Order_Status == "In Progress"].count();
		completedCount = Sales_Order[Order_Status == "Production Complete"].count();
		qcCount = Sales_Order[Order_Status == "Checking Passed"].count();
		packedCount = Sales_Order[Order_Status == "Packed"].count();
		dispatchedCount = Sales_Order[Order_Status == "Dispatched"].count();
	}
	catch (pipelineError)
	{
		// Keep the report response valid; the visible zeroes indicate that this
		// particular count query could not be read.
	}

	try
	{
		dTxt = ifnull(dateTxt,"").toString().trim();
		if(dTxt == "")
		{
			dDay = zoho.currentdate;
		}
		else
		{
			dDay = dTxt.toDate();
		}
		dayTxt = dDay.toString("dd-MMM-yyyy");

		//----------------------------------------------------------------------
		// The employee directory, in ONE query.
		//
		// Every employee, not only the active ones: a person made Inactive last
		// week still did the work that is on last week's logs, and a report that
		// silently drops their rows makes the day's totals disagree with itself.
		// The Status field travels with each row so the screen can say so.
		//----------------------------------------------------------------------
		// sort by on its own assignment - inline in a for-each header it does not
		// reliably parse, and a function that will not save is indistinguishable
		// from one that saved and did nothing.
		allEmps = Employee[ID != 0] sort by Employee_Name;

		empName = Map();
		empRole = Map();
		empStat = Map();
		empOrder = List();

		for each  emp in allEmps
		{
			eKey = emp.ID.toString();
			eNm = ifnull(emp.Employee_Name,"").toString().trim().replaceAll("\"","'");
			if(eNm == "")
			{
				eNm = "Employee " + eKey;
			}
			empName.put(eKey,eNm);
			empRole.put(eKey,ifnull(emp.Designation,"").toString().trim().replaceAll("\"","'"));
			empStat.put(eKey,ifnull(emp.Status,"").toString().trim());
			empOrder.add(eKey);
		}

		//----------------------------------------------------------------------
		// The day's stage logs. Bounded by Log_Date, which is what keeps this
		// safe however long the app has been running.
		//
		// BOTH statuses. A stage still running is work in hand - he is standing
		// at the machine right now - and a report that only counted finished
		// stages would show an empty row for the man halfway through a long cut.
		// It is counted separately from finished work and contributes no output,
		// because it has none yet.
		//
		// Ascending on Added_Time: the stamp is written at Start, so this is the
		// order the shift actually happened in. Log_Date is identical on every
		// row of a one-day view and can order nothing.
		//----------------------------------------------------------------------
		//----------------------------------------------------------------------
		// A STAGE IS SPLIT BETWEEN SEVERAL OPERATORS, so the unit of one person's
		// day is a SHARE, not a stage.
		//
		// Both sources are read and flattened into one list of rows before any
		// counting happens:
		//
		//   Stage_Assignment - one row per operator per stage. This is what the
		//   floor produces now.
		//
		//   Stage_Log - the stage header. Counted ONLY when it has no shares
		//   hanging off it, which is every stage logged before the split existed
		//   and every outsourced one. Counting a split stage's header as well
		//   would report its pieces twice: once against the header, once against
		//   the people who actually did the work.
		//
		// The unassigned remainder of a split stage - pieces the supervisor never
		// handed to anybody - is deliberately in NOBODY's row. It is a fact about
		// the stage, not about a person, and this report only answers questions
		// about people.
		//----------------------------------------------------------------------
		dayShares = Stage_Assignment[Log_Date == dDay] sort by Added_Time;
		dayLogs = Stage_Log[Log_Date == dDay] sort by Added_Time;

		// Which stage headers have been split. Filled from the shares, read once
		// per header below - so telling a split stage from a whole one costs no
		// query at all.
		splitLogs = Map();
		workRows = List();

		for each  agD in dayShares
		{
			agRow = Map();

			agOpKey = "0";
			if(agD.Operator != null)
			{
				agOpKey = agD.Operator.toString();
			}
			agRow.put("op",agOpKey);

			agPlanKey = "";
			if(agD.Plan != null)
			{
				agPlanKey = agD.Plan.toString();
			}
			agRow.put("plan",agPlanKey);

			agItemKey = "";
			if(agD.Plan_Item != null)
			{
				agItemKey = agD.Plan_Item.toString();
			}
			agRow.put("item",agItemKey);

			if(agD.Stage_Log != null)
			{
				splitLogs.put(agD.Stage_Log.toString(),"1");
			}

			agRow.put("phase",ifnull(agD.Phase_Name,"").toString());

			// Qty_In of a share is what the man was HANDED. Same meaning the
			// stage's own Qty_In has, one level down.
			agRow.put("qtyIn",ifnull(agD.Assigned_Qty,0));
			agRow.put("qtyOut",ifnull(agD.Qty_Out,0));
			agRow.put("status",ifnull(agD.Assignment_Status,"").toString().trim());
			agRow.put("start",ifnull(agD.Start_Time,"").toString());
			agRow.put("end",ifnull(agD.End_Time,"").toString());
			agRow.put("outsourced","false");
			agRow.put("remarks",ifnull(agD.Remarks,"").toString());

			workRows.add(agRow);
		}

		for each  lgD in dayLogs
		{
			// Skipped when the stage was split - its people are already above.
			if(splitLogs.get(lgD.ID.toString()) == null)
			{
				lgRow = Map();

				lgOpKey = "0";
				if(lgD.Operator != null)
				{
					lgOpKey = lgD.Operator.toString();
				}
				lgRow.put("op",lgOpKey);

				lgPlanKey = "";
				if(lgD.Plan != null)
				{
					lgPlanKey = lgD.Plan.toString();
				}
				lgRow.put("plan",lgPlanKey);

				lgItemKey = "";
				if(lgD.Plan_Item != null)
				{
					lgItemKey = lgD.Plan_Item.toString();
				}
				lgRow.put("item",lgItemKey);

				lgRow.put("phase",ifnull(lgD.Phase_Name,"").toString());
				lgRow.put("qtyIn",ifnull(lgD.Qty_In,0));
				lgRow.put("qtyOut",ifnull(lgD.Qty_Out,0));
				lgRow.put("status",ifnull(lgD.Stage_Status,"").toString().trim());
				lgRow.put("start",ifnull(lgD.Start_Time,"").toString());
				lgRow.put("end",ifnull(lgD.End_Time,"").toString());

				lgOutTxt = "false";
				if(ifnull(lgD.Is_Outsourced,false) == true)
				{
					lgOutTxt = "true";
				}
				lgRow.put("outsourced",lgOutTxt);
				lgRow.put("remarks",ifnull(lgD.Remarks,"").toString());

				workRows.add(lgRow);
			}
		}

		// Caches. A miss is STORED as an empty string rather than left absent, so
		// a plan with no sales order attached is not re-queried on every one of
		// its rows.
		planNoM = Map();
		planSoM = Map();
		planSoStatM = Map();
		itemNmM = Map();

		// Per-person accumulators, all keyed on the operator's record id as text.
		opKeys = List();
		opDone = Map();
		opOpen = Map();
		opIn = Map();
		opHand = Map();
		opOut = Map();
		opRows = Map();
		opSoSeen = Map();
		opSoJson = Map();

		// Iterating the flattened list, not a query result. A share and an
		// unsplit stage are the same shape by the time they get here, which is
		// the whole point of building it.
		for each  wr in workRows
		{
			// "0" is the operator that was never recorded - a stage opened before
			// anyone was put on it, or an outsourced one. That work has to appear
			// somewhere or the day's totals stop matching the sum of the rows.
			oKey = ifnull(wr.get("op"),"0").toString();
			if(oKey == "")
			{
				oKey = "0";
			}

			if(opDone.get(oKey) == null)
			{
				opKeys.add(oKey);
				opDone.put(oKey,0);
				opOpen.put(oKey,0);
				opIn.put(oKey,0);
				opHand.put(oKey,0);
				opOut.put(oKey,0);
				opRows.put(oKey,"");
				opSoSeen.put(oKey,"");
				opSoJson.put(oKey,"");
			}

			//------------------------------------------------------------------
			// The plan behind the work, and the order behind the plan.
			//
			// The row carries the plan's id as text, so the cache is keyed and
			// queried off the same string whichever source the row came from.
			//------------------------------------------------------------------
			pKey = ifnull(wr.get("plan"),"").toString();

			if(pKey != "" && planNoM.get(pKey) == null)
			{
				pNo = "";
				pSo = "";
				pSoStat = "";
				for each  plr in Production_Planning[ID == pKey.toLong()]
				{
					pNo = ifnull(plr.Plan_No,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'");
					for each  sor in Sales_Order[ID == plr.Sales_Order]
					{
						pSo = ifnull(sor.Sales_Order,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'");
						pSoStat = ifnull(sor.Order_Status,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'");
					}
				}
				planNoM.put(pKey,pNo);
				planSoM.put(pKey,pSo);
				planSoStatM.put(pKey,pSoStat);
			}

			planNoTxt = "";
			soTxt = "";
			soStatTxt = "";
			if(pKey != "")
			{
				planNoTxt = ifnull(planNoM.get(pKey),"").toString();
				soTxt = ifnull(planSoM.get(pKey),"").toString();
				soStatTxt = ifnull(planSoStatM.get(pKey),"").toString();
			}

			//------------------------------------------------------------------
			// Plan_Item is a LOOKUP, so the row holds the record id. The old
			// function put that id straight into the payload as "Item"; the name
			// lives on the Plan_Item record.
			//------------------------------------------------------------------
			iKey = ifnull(wr.get("item"),"").toString();

			if(iKey != "" && itemNmM.get(iKey) == null)
			{
				iNm = "";
				for each  pit in Plan_Item[ID == iKey.toLong()]
				{
					iNm = ifnull(pit.Item_Name,"").toString().trim().replaceAll("\"","'");
				}
				itemNmM.put(iKey,iNm);
			}

			itemTxt = "";
			if(iKey != "")
			{
				itemTxt = ifnull(itemNmM.get(iKey),"").toString();
			}

			//------------------------------------------------------------------
			// Creator fields that were never written are EMPTY, not null, and
			// toDecimal() throws on empty - normalise through a string first.
			//------------------------------------------------------------------
			qiS = ifnull(wr.get("qtyIn"),"0").toString().trim();
			if(qiS == "")
			{
				qiS = "0";
			}
			qoS = ifnull(wr.get("qtyOut"),"0").toString().trim();
			if(qoS == "")
			{
				qoS = "0";
			}
			qIn = qiS.toDecimal().toLong();
			qOut = qoS.toDecimal().toLong();

			stat = ifnull(wr.get("status"),"").toString().trim();

			//------------------------------------------------------------------
			// THE INPUT OF A FINISHED STAGE AND THE INPUT OF A RUNNING ONE ARE
			// KEPT APART, and the reason is the loss figure.
			//
			// Loss is piecesIn minus piecesOut. A running stage has taken pieces
			// in and produced nothing yet - Qty_Out is only written at End - so
			// folding its input into the same total would report every piece
			// currently on the machine as lost. The man halfway through a 200
			// piece cut would read "200 lost" until he finished.
			//
			// So Done contributes to piecesIn, running contributes to
			// piecesInHand, and the two are shown as the different things they
			// are: what he got through, and what he is still holding.
			//------------------------------------------------------------------
			if(stat == "Done")
			{
				opDone.put(oKey,opDone.get(oKey) + 1);
				opIn.put(oKey,opIn.get(oKey) + qIn);
				opOut.put(oKey,opOut.get(oKey) + qOut);
				totStagesDone = totStagesDone + 1;
				totIn = totIn + qIn;
				totOut = totOut + qOut;
			}
			else
			{
				opOpen.put(oKey,opOpen.get(oKey) + 1);
				opHand.put(oKey,opHand.get(oKey) + qIn);
				totStagesOpen = totStagesOpen + 1;
				totOpen = totOpen + qIn;
			}

			// Distinct sales orders this person touched. Wrapped in pipes so a
			// contains() test cannot match a number inside a longer one.
			if(soTxt != "")
			{
				seenTxt = ifnull(opSoSeen.get(oKey),"").toString();
				if(seenTxt.contains("|" + soTxt + "|") == false)
				{
					opSoSeen.put(oKey,seenTxt + "|" + soTxt + "|");
					soAcc = ifnull(opSoJson.get(oKey),"").toString();
					if(soAcc != "")
					{
						soAcc = soAcc + ",";
					}
					opSoJson.put(oKey,soAcc + "\"" + soTxt + "\"");
				}
			}

			outsTxt = ifnull(wr.get("outsourced"),"false").toString();
			if(outsTxt != "true")
			{
				outsTxt = "false";
			}

			// Times go out as the raw strings Creator holds. The duration is
			// worked out in the widget instead, where the several shapes a Time
			// field can render as ("09:00", "09:00:00", "09:00 AM") can be parsed
			// defensively and an unreadable one shown as a dash rather than
			// turned into a confident wrong number.
			rowJ = "{\"phase\":\"" + ifnull(wr.get("phase"),"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\",\"item\":\"" + itemTxt + "\",\"planNo\":\"" + planNoTxt + "\",\"salesOrder\":\"" + soTxt + "\",\"salesOrderStatus\":\"" + soStatTxt + "\",\"qtyIn\":" + qIn + ",\"qtyOut\":" + qOut + ",\"start\":\"" + ifnull(wr.get("start"),"").toString() + "\",\"end\":\"" + ifnull(wr.get("end"),"").toString() + "\",\"status\":\"" + stat + "\",\"outsourced\":" + outsTxt + ",\"remarks\":\"" + ifnull(wr.get("remarks"),"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\"}";

			prevRows = ifnull(opRows.get(oKey),"").toString();
			if(prevRows != "")
			{
				prevRows = prevRows + ",";
			}
			opRows.put(oKey,prevRows + rowJ);
		}

		//----------------------------------------------------------------------
		// One object per person who worked.
		//----------------------------------------------------------------------
		for each  wk in opKeys
		{
			wNm = "Not recorded";
			wRole = "";
			wStat = "";
			unattTxt = "true";

			if(wk != "0")
			{
				unattTxt = "false";
				totPeople = totPeople + 1;
				wNm = ifnull(empName.get(wk),"").toString();
				wRole = ifnull(empRole.get(wk),"").toString();
				wStat = ifnull(empStat.get(wk),"").toString();

				// An operator deleted from Employee since the log was written.
				// The work still happened, so the row stays and says what it can.
				if(wNm == "")
				{
					wNm = "Employee " + wk;
				}
			}

			if(workedJson != "")
			{
				workedJson = workedJson + ",";
			}
			// IDs forced to string - 18-digit ids break JSON.parse otherwise.
			workedJson = workedJson + "{\"id\":\"" + wk + "\",\"name\":\"" + wNm + "\",\"role\":\"" + wRole + "\",\"status\":\"" + wStat + "\",\"unattributed\":" + unattTxt + ",\"stagesDone\":" + opDone.get(wk) + ",\"stagesOpen\":" + opOpen.get(wk) + ",\"piecesIn\":" + opIn.get(wk) + ",\"piecesInHand\":" + opHand.get(wk) + ",\"piecesOut\":" + opOut.get(wk) + ",\"orders\":[" + ifnull(opSoJson.get(wk),"").toString() + "],\"stages\":[" + ifnull(opRows.get(wk),"").toString() + "]}";
		}

		//----------------------------------------------------------------------
		// Everyone ACTIVE who logged nothing that day.
		//
		// Not called idle, and not counted into anything. Most of this list is
		// store, wash and packing staff who never write a Stage_Log at all - the
		// screen says so rather than implying they did nothing. It answers one
		// question only: is somebody's work missing from the report?
		//
		// Inactive employees are left out. A person who has left the company
		// showing up every day as "nothing logged" is noise, and their past work
		// still appears above on the days they did it.
		//----------------------------------------------------------------------
		for each  ik in empOrder
		{
			if(opDone.get(ik) == null && ifnull(empStat.get(ik),"").toString() == "Active")
			{
				if(idleJson != "")
				{
					idleJson = idleJson + ",";
				}
				idleJson = idleJson + "{\"id\":\"" + ik + "\",\"name\":\"" + ifnull(empName.get(ik),"").toString() + "\",\"role\":\"" + ifnull(empRole.get(ik),"").toString() + "\"}";
			}
		}
	}
	catch (e)
	{
		// Creator collapses every runtime failure to a widget into a generic
		// "code 9430", so the real message is carried back in the payload.
		errJson = "\"DELUGE: " + e.toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\"";
	}

	// Built by hand. Map.toString() stops emitting valid JSON the moment the
	// structure nests Map -> List -> Map, which is exactly the stages array, and
	// List.toString() does not wrap in []. That is what the old function did.
	return "{\"errors\":[" + errJson + "],\"date\":\"" + dayTxt + "\",\"worked\":[" + workedJson + "],\"noLogs\":[" + idleJson + "],\"totals\":{\"people\":" + totPeople + ",\"stagesDone\":" + totStagesDone + ",\"stagesOpen\":" + totStagesOpen + ",\"piecesIn\":" + totIn + ",\"piecesInHand\":" + totOpen + ",\"piecesOut\":" + totOut + "},\"pipeline\":{\"pending\":" + pendingCount + ",\"inProgress\":" + inProgressCount + ",\"completed\":" + completedCount + ",\"qcPassed\":" + qcCount + ",\"packed\":" + packedCount + ",\"dispatched\":" + dispatchedCount + "}}";
}
